sentences.t:
“Saludadme a mio Çid, el que en buen[a] çinxo espada;
sean las vistas destas tres se[d]manas;
s' yo bivo so, allí iré sin falla.”

sentences.l:
“Greet-me to my Çid, he who in good (hour) girded sword;
let-be the meeting of-these three weeks;
if I alive am, there I-will-go without fail.”

sentences.t:
Con el rey van leoneses e mesnadas gallizianas,
non son en cuenta, sabet, las castellanas;
sueltan las riendas, a las vistas se van adeliñadas.

sentences.l:
With the king go Leonese and households Galician,
not are in count, know-ye, the Castilian;
they-loosen the reins, to the meeting themselves they-go straight-directed.

sentences.t:
dentro es su mugier e sus fijas amas a dos,
en que tiene su alma e s[o] coraçón,
e otras dueñas que las sirven a su sabor;

sentences.l:
inside is his wife and his daughters both two,
in whom he-holds his soul and h[is] heart,
and other ladies who them serve to their liking;
